Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
PROGRAMMING IN MATHEMATICA

29 Jan 2003\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["Procedural Programming Style", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports a variety of so-called programming styles.  You can program in \
the spirit of C or Fortran; you may also do object oriented programming like \
C++; or you can do functional programming like LISP; and the rule-based \
programming style is powerful and can be concise.  Here we demonstrate few \
ways of writing a factorial function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[m_Integer] := 
      Module[\ {i, \ n}, \ \n\t
        If[\ m\  < \ 0, \ \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tReturn[Infinity]]; \n\t
        n\  = \ 1; \n\t
        For[i\  = \ 1, \ i\  <= \ m, \ \(++i\), \ \[IndentingNewLine]\t
          n\  = \ n*i]; \[IndentingNewLine]\ \ \ \ \ n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Few fine points: (1) use the input argument m only as a constant, (2) declare \
all variables except the argument as local, (3) return value is the last \
expression.  (4) Notice the syntax of If[  ], and For[  ] statements.
We use the function as usual:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
A much quicker way is to used the build-in function n! (of course).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ \(n!\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Or use a recursive definition", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[0]\  = \ 1; \ fac[n_Integer]\  := n\ *\ fac[n - 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Or use functional programming style:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ Times\  @@ \ Range[n]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Procedural Programming Constructs", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Apart from syntactic differences, many of these constructs are the same as \
C.  The ",
  StyleBox["For",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " loop:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(For[initial, \ condition, \ 
      inc, \[IndentingNewLine]\ \ \ \ body]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(For[i\  = \ 0, \ 
      i\  < \ 4, \ \(++i\), \[IndentingNewLine]\ \ \ Print["\<i=\>", \ 
        i]\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("i="\[InvisibleSpace]0\),
      SequenceForm[ "i=", 0],
      Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("i="\[InvisibleSpace]1\),
      SequenceForm[ "i=", 1],
      Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("i="\[InvisibleSpace]2\),
      SequenceForm[ "i=", 2],
      Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("i="\[InvisibleSpace]3\),
      SequenceForm[ "i=", 3],
      Editable->False]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(i\  = \ 3; \ While[\ i\  > 0, \ \(--i\); \ Print[i]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0\)], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Which\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Which[test1, value1, test2, value2, ... ] evaluates each of the testi \
in turn, returning the value of the valuei corresponding to the first one \
that yields True."\)], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Which is like a sequence of if-else.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x_]\  := \ 
      Which[x < \(-1\), \ \(-1\), \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx <= 0, \ \(-1\)/
          2, \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx > 0\  && \ x\  < 1, \ 
        1/2, \[IndentingNewLine]\t\tx\  >= \ 1, \ 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[f[x], \ {x, \(-3\), 3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[GraphicsData["PostScript", "\<\
